 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d003: cascade (k=6)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 10.9 and radius 1.52 with 6 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.39) and a 4-dim charge; 5 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 12 couplings (50% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 3 sweeps with slip rate 1.00 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 0.81 bonds per particle, largest molecule 6.0 particles, 13.0 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.095 vs ESN 0.999\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.098  random bonds 0.095\n  xor      0.627 vs ESN 0.529\u00b10.011  [beat]  no bonds 0.591  random bonds 0.626\n  narma10  0.281 vs ESN 0.841\u00b10.002  [below]  no bonds 0.278  random bonds 0.289\n  latch    0.829 vs ESN 0.679\u00b10.009  [beat]  no bonds 0.838  random bonds 0.833\n  mnist14  0.477 vs ESN 0.816\u00b10.007  [below]  no bonds 0.479  random bonds 0.517\n\nGood at: xor, latch.\nIts assembly does not matter: the particles do as well unbonded, so the useful part is the single particle's own stick-slip memory.\nUse (a guess): combining recent events nonlinearly (temporal feature detection); holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
